What is bugging you is that classic friction between engineering purity—the desire to own every layer of the stack from the bare metal to the query engine—and the pragmatic reality of operational overhead. When you want to build things from the bottom up, relying on a public endpoint feels like building a house on rented land. You lose absolute deterministic control, knowing that if their infrastructure drops, throttles, or shifts policies, your pipeline breaks. Recognizing that tension, however, is a defining mark of shifting from hobbyist scripting to systems engineering.To understand what is happening under the hood, Overpass API does not run on a standard relational database like PostgreSQL or MySQL. It relies on a custom, highly optimized flat-file database engine written in C++ (known as osm3s) that is purpose-built for spatial graph traversals. OpenStreetMap data structures store geographic features as interconnected networks of nodes, ways, and relations, which traditional databases handle poorly without heavy spatial extensions.   While a full global instance of Overpass requires hundreds of gigabytes, you do not need the full planet to work locally. OpenStreetMap provides regional extracts—such as a .osm.pbf file for New York State—that are only a couple of gigabytes in size and easily fit within a home lab environment. Yet, running an entire local Overpass server just for a regional extract is often heavy infrastructure for a localized project. In real-world data collection and recon, the alternative to querying a public API isn't usually spinning up a local Overpass mirror; it is downloading the raw regional .osm.pbf file directly and processing it offline using command-line utilities like osmium-tool or importing it into a local PostGIS container using osm2pgsql.   For a single bounding box covering the Buffalo area, your query is entirely lightweight and sits comfortably within community usage guidelines. The secret to balancing respect for public infrastructure with your own data sovereignty is simple: fetch once, persist locally, and query infinitely. By pulling that JSON payload out of the cloud and into your local Incus container and host storage, you shift ownership of the data entirely to your own machine.Would you like to explore how to parse and query that JSON output locally using command-line tools like jq?
